# THE OTHER PATH — adopting the live cluster instead of creating an ephemeral one.
#
# Not `.tf`, so Terraform does not read it. Renaming it is a deliberate act, which is the
# correct amount of friction for what it does.
#
# ============================================================================
# READ THIS BEFORE RENAMING
# ============================================================================
#
# Converting `respect-the-funk` to three-region Standard is a ONE-WAY DOOR.
#
#   * Basic -> Standard is reversible. Standard -> Basic is supported and in-place, with
#     a 48-hour lockout before you can go back up to Standard again.
#   * Adding a region is NOT reversible. CockroachDB Cloud does not support removing a
#     region from a Basic or Standard cluster, at all, ever. Verified from the Cloud
#     documentation on 2026-08-13:
#     https://www.cockroachlabs.com/docs/cockroachcloud/basic-cluster-management
#     — "You cannot remove a region once it has been added."
#   * The documented way back to a single-region cluster is therefore: back up, create a
#     new single-region cluster, restore, repoint every DATABASE_URL, delete the old one.
#     That is a data migration, not a config change.
#
# So: the demo does not run here. This block exists for the day the product wants
# residency in production, which is a decision with a customer behind it and not a
# decision made on the afternoon of a deadline.
#
# ============================================================================
#
# Two further things that are UNVERIFIED and would be found out the hard way:
#
#   1. Whether the provider treats a BASIC -> STANDARD change of `plan` as an in-place
#      update or as a replacement. If it is a replacement, `terraform apply` DESTROYS
#      the live cluster and creates a new empty one. The provider schema does not record
#      RequiresReplace in a form that can be read without applying, so the only safe
#      procedure is: run `terraform plan`, read it, and abort on the words
#      "must be replaced" or "forces replacement". `prevent_destroy` below turns that
#      from a judgement call into a hard error.
#   2. Whether the region configuration of a cluster that was created single-region can
#      be edited at all. The Basic management docs say "You cannot currently edit the
#      region configuration for a single-region cluster once it has been created", which
#      may be a console limitation or may be a platform one. If it is a platform one,
#      this whole path is closed and only backup/restore remains.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# import {
#   to = cockroach_cluster.live
#   id = "ae38b92e-c1ad-4a06-a247-489cd5ce9964"
# }
#
# resource "cockroach_cluster" "live" {
#   name           = "respect-the-funk"
#   cloud_provider = "AWS"
#   plan           = "STANDARD"
#
#   regions = [
#     { name = "us-east-1", primary = true },
#     { name = "eu-west-1", primary = false },
#     { name = "ap-southeast-1", primary = false },
#   ]
#
#   serverless = {
#     upgrade_type = "AUTOMATIC"
#     usage_limits = {
#       provisioned_virtual_cpus = 2
#       storage_mib_limit        = 81920
#     }
#   }
#
#   delete_protection = true
#
#   lifecycle {
#     # The point of this module is a plan change. If Terraform decides the only way to
#     # achieve it is to replace the resource, that is a destroyed production database,
#     # and this turns it into a refusal at plan time rather than a discovery at apply
#     # time.
#     prevent_destroy = true
#   }
# }
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The equivalent by hand, which is what docs/runbooks/multiregion.md §7 actually
# recommends for this path — the Cloud API's plan change is documented as zero-downtime
# and the ordering is explicit, where Terraform would decide the ordering itself:
#
#   ccloud cluster update respect-the-funk --provisioned-vcpus 2
#   ccloud cluster update respect-the-funk us-east-1 eu-west-1 ap-southeast-1 \
#          --primary-region us-east-1
#
# Both flags verified present in ccloud 0.8.23 (`ccloud cluster update --help`,
# 2026-08-13). Neither has been run.
